+{"cell_id": "sha256:1515b1f5d4476d91a49446a1d81d2b5341b39e47ba9bed1f3afcbb67822e8461", "chat_messages": [{"content": "You are writing one Python solution for a programming task. Return only raw Python code or one fenced python block. Do not explain the answer.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Seed cell: sha256:1515b1f5d4476d91a49446a1d81d2b5341b39e47ba9bed1f3afcbb67822e8461\nStrategy: invariant_first\nBefore writing code, identify the key invariant or recurrence and then encode it compactly. Return only the final Python code.\n\nTitle: Online Shopping\nAtCoder Inc. sells merchandise through its online shop.\nTakahashi has decided to purchase N types of products from there.\nFor each integer i from 1 to N, the i-th type of product has a price of P_i yen each, and he will buy Q_i of this.\nAdditionally, he must pay a shipping fee.\nThe shipping fee is 0 yen if the total price of the products purchased is S yen or above, and K yen otherwise.\nHe will pay the total price of the products purchased plus the shipping fee.\nCalculate the amount he will pay.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN S K\nP_1 Q_1\nP_2 Q_2\n\\vdots\nP_N Q_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the amount Takahashi will pay for online shopping.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq 100\n- 1\\leq S\\leq 10000\n- 1\\leq K\\leq 10000\n- 1\\leq P_i\\leq 10000\n- 1\\leq Q_i\\leq 100\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n2 2000 500\n1000 1\n100 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2100\n\nTakahashi buys one product for 1000 yen and six products for 100 yen each.\nThus, the total price of the products is 1000\\times 1+100\\times 6=1600 yen.\nSince the total amount for the products is less than 2000 yen, the shipping fee will be 500 yen.\nTherefore, the amount Takahashi will pay is 1600+500=2100 yen.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 2000 500\n1000 1\n100 6\n5000 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n6600\n\nThe total price of the products is 1000\\times 1+100\\times 6+5000\\times 1=6600 yen.\nSince the total amount for the products is not less than 2000 yen, the shipping fee will be 0 yen.\nTherefore, the amount Takahashi will pay is 6600+0=6600 yen.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n2 2000 500\n1000 1\n1000 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\n2000\n\nThere may be multiple products with the same price per item.\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:86a0cef0ca52f280058823303b4ecf7b86b436ce676ed41bc793d485afb7cffe", "code_extraction": {"method": "fenced_code_block", "status": "ok"}, "completion_token_count": 183, "content_hash": "sha256:fb14ddb72748dcd2d196cf297ef0b9ab943a22810e786a9c646c3aa48c824c7d", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "import sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    S = int(input_data[1])\n    K = int(input_data[2])\n    \n    total_price = 0\n    idx = 3\n    for _ in range(N):\n        P = int(input_data[idx])\n        Q = int(input_data[idx+1])\n        total_price += P * Q\n        idx += 2\n        \n    shipping_fee = K if total_price < S else 0\n    print(total_price + shipping_fee)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:06c42a08d62d982b16c64094f3f3b65539cf10c4d7bebc0358c9a386fb14d523", "finish_reason": "stop", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "gemma4", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-GGUF", "model_name": "Gemma4-26B-A4B-it", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-GGUF/snapshots/3365c68df1a83799b846d05324ebfadbb8cc70b3/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf", "model_quantization": "UD-Q4_K_M", "model_revision": "3365c68df1a83799b846d05324ebfadbb8cc70b3", "n_ctx": 4096, "order_index": 13, "private_test_sha256": "sha256:c5bd7222951fca18baba0cc8c93e3b39dfd61742cf8ef5c97193f50ee0c206c9", "prompt_sha256": "sha256:2766b2add9081b8bc62314922a57d5e83a4fce4deaca1bc295214927ecc1f268", "prompt_token_count": 758, "public_test_sha256": "sha256:f9b556ce4ce0362b6ecace9761fb034e106e6a2e6d4d138485fd02db233c93d6", "raw_generation_error": null, "raw_sealed_at": "2026-08-07T15:42:02Z", "raw_stdout": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    S = int(input_data[1])\n    K = int(input_data[2])\n    \n    total_price = 0\n    idx = 3\n    for _ in range(N):\n        P = int(input_data[idx])\n        Q = int(input_data[idx+1])\n        total_price += P * Q\n        idx += 2\n        \n    shipping_fee = K if total_price < S else 0\n    print(total_price + shipping_fee)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```", "raw_stdout_sha256": "sha256:f443d00d4cfb0328228dc65d97b1f7bbdb0eedbbcad8be88a8cfddcc874e5e34", "refusal": false, "repeat_penalty": 1.05, "row_hash": "sha256:a46eda3c896516581624e463399965220f3c3b126e06f637539b29e13b31e406", "run_date": "20260807", "runtime": "python_stdio", "schema": "carnot.experiment_6192.live_strategy_seed_stream.v1.raw_row", "seed": 619200013, "selector_features": {"date_bucket": "2023-Q4", "difficulty": "easy", "platform": "atcoder", "prompt_size_bucket": "medium", "supported_runtime": "python_stdio", "tag_bucket": "untagged"}, "split": "csl_seed", "stable_task_hash": "sha256:052f70e02964a1fbd0472eae9ab122541f2692d70e73d7558024bec6997a3679", "strategy_id": "invariant_first", "strategy_prompt_sha256": "sha256:973248f9221cfafcf6460deacb3340f199948114a56709b5c14712108209d92e", "task_id": "abc332_a", "temperature": 0.0, "timeout": false, "timing": {"decode_time_s": 1.813805, "started_monotonic_s": 1213097.94338}, "top_k": 40, "top_p": 1.0, "transport_failure": false, "truncated": false}
+{"cell_id": "sha256:6eda5c7543d8fbeb78b85da1bffd3d38616b84f313314b1374edae1aa8308df3", "chat_messages": [{"content": "You are writing one Python solution for a programming task. Return only raw Python code or one fenced python block. Do not explain the answer.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Seed cell: sha256:6eda5c7543d8fbeb78b85da1bffd3d38616b84f313314b1374edae1aa8308df3\nStrategy: invariant_first\nBefore writing code, identify the key invariant or recurrence and then encode it compactly. Return only the final Python code.\n\nTitle: Chords\nThere are 2N points placed at equal intervals on a circle, numbered 1 to 2N in a clockwise direction starting from a certain point.\nThere are also N chords on the circle, with the i-th chord connecting points A_i and B_i.\nIt is guaranteed that all the values A_1,\\dots,A_N,B_1,\\dots,B_N are distinct.\nDetermine whether there is an intersection between the chords.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nA_1 B_1\nA_2 B_2\n\\vdots\nA_N B_N\n\nOutput\n\nIf there is an intersection between the chords, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2\\leq N \\leq 2\\times 10^5\n- 1\\leq A_i,B_i \\leq 2N\n- A_1,\\dots,A_N,B_1,\\dots,B_N are all distinct\n- All input values are integers\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\n1 3\n4 2\n5 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\n\nAs shown in the figure, chord 1 (the line segment connecting points 1 and 3) and chord 2 (the line segment connecting points 4 and 2) intersect, so print Yes.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3\n6 1\n4 3\n2 5\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\n\nAs shown in the figure, there is no intersection between the chords, so print No.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n4\n2 4\n3 7\n8 6\n5 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\nYes\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:e0c3a89af5176617018f9fb92412a2fbb34272f3f63beb11b9da1e3d067757da", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:5e367b93157c434a8a7a3d0602ca32cff28f58325258b93a9184625dfa75415d", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks us to determine if any two chords intersect on a circle.\nThere are $2N$ points on a circle, numbered 1 to $2N$ clockwise.\nWe are given $N$ chords, each connecting two distinct points. All endpoints are distinct.\nTwo chords $(A_i, B_i)$ and $(A_j, B_j)$ intersect if and only if their endpoints alternate around the circle.\nThat is, if we traverse the circle from 1 to $2N$, the order of endpoints for an intersecting pair must be like $A_i, A_j, B_i, B_j$ or $A_i, B_j, B_i, A_j$, etc., assuming $A_i < B_i$ and $A_j < B_j$ after sorting each chord's endpoints.\nMore formally, let's normalize each chord such that $L_i = \\min(A_i, B_i)$ and $R_i = \\max(A_i, B_i)$.\nTwo chords $(L_i, R_i)$ and $(L_j, R_j)$ intersect if and only if $L_i < L_j < R_i < R_j$ or $L_j < L_i < R_j < R_i$.\nThis is a classic problem that can be solved using a stack or by checking intervals.\nSince $N$ is up to $2 \\times 10^5$, an $O(N^2)$ solution will be too slow. We need $O(N \\log N)$ or $O(N)$.\n\nLet's process the points in order from 1 to $2N$.\nWe can maintain a stack of active chords. When we encounter the first endpoint of a chord, we push it onto the stack. When we encounter the second endpoint of a chord, we check if it matches the top of the stack.", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:0e138e06670b8c09421952b074e9e9435e6f51faa14d28b7eacf910b59801e78", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "qwen3", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF", "model_name": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF/snapshots/a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf", "model_quantization": "UD-Q4_K_M", "model_revision": "a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c", "n_ctx": 4096, "order_index": 14, "private_test_sha256": "sha256:3e245b8fdf040942cbb7da974c2fd33dc800ff039a