+{"cell_id": "sha256:109b984b88f26435330fff39a8f99b040f09f1bb8690e2f01beac81f390233eb", "chat_messages": [{"content": "You are writing one Python solution for a programming task. Return only raw Python code or one fenced python block. Do not explain the answer.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Seed cell: sha256:109b984b88f26435330fff39a8f99b040f09f1bb8690e2f01beac81f390233eb\nStrategy: invariant_first\nBefore writing code, identify the key invariant or recurrence and then encode it compactly. Return only the final Python code.\n\nTitle: B. Good Kid\nSlavic is preparing a present for a friend's birthday. He has an array $a$ of $n$ digits and the present will be the product of all these digits. Because Slavic is a good kid who wants to make the biggest product possible, he wants to add $1$ to exactly one of his digits. \n\nWhat is the maximum product Slavic can make?\n\nInput\n\nThe first line contains a single integer $t$ ($1 \\leq t \\leq 10^4$)\u00a0\u2014 the number of test cases.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains a single integer $n$ ($1 \\leq n \\leq 9$)\u00a0\u2014 the number of digits.\n\nThe second line of each test case contains $n$ space-separated integers $a_i$ ($0 \\leq a_i \\leq 9$)\u00a0\u2014 the digits in the array.\n\nOutput\n\nFor each test case, output a single integer\u00a0\u2014 the maximum product Slavic can make, by adding $1$ to exactly one of his digits.Sample Input 1:\n4\n\n4\n\n2 2 1 2\n\n3\n\n0 1 2\n\n5\n\n4 3 2 3 4\n\n9\n\n9 9 9 9 9 9 9 9 9\n\n\n\nSample Output 1:\n\n16\n2\n432\n430467210\n\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:5be213819ec1e077a9fe1ccd881eed52c5fb5b12f24d6c629d57d519a33a59c6", "code_extraction": {"method": "fenced_code_block", "status": "ok"}, "completion_token_count": 269, "content_hash": "sha256:af5604a648b89d12aa0f49a465f28b63a6395949f64f093b56d81dd9cbb79696", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "import sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    t = int(input_data[0])\n    idx = 1\n    results = []\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        n = int(input_data[idx])\n        idx += 1\n        a = [int(x) for x in input_data[idx : idx + n]]\n        idx += n\n        \n        # To maximize the product when adding 1 to exactly one element,\n        # we should add 1 to the smallest element.\n        # This is because (x+1)/x is largest when x is smallest.\n        \n        min_val = min(a)\n        min_idx = a.index(min_val)\n        \n        a[min_idx] += 1\n        \n        product = 1\n        for val in a:\n            product *= val\n        results.append(str(product))\n        \n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\".join(results) + \"\\n\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:e0681eb1ebc474f1bde3a8f847e8faf2b3ff65cd61797effa62d7629df6e8cc0", "finish_reason": "stop", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "gemma4", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-GGUF", "model_name": "Gemma4-26B-A4B-it", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-GGUF/snapshots/3365c68df1a83799b846d05324ebfadbb8cc70b3/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf", "model_quantization": "UD-Q4_K_M", "model_revision": "3365c68df1a83799b846d05324ebfadbb8cc70b3", "n_ctx": 4096, "order_index": 15, "private_test_sha256": "sha256:5d2f0f910b4e479db9678a3936ca3c6f84c8fd062b60e74e98453ba9bc391050", "prompt_sha256": "sha256:3a2059742d1b0f29f5d06e59e94b0023c600efa0b418d4029f91051e45449d14", "prompt_token_count": 453, "public_test_sha256": "sha256:4d644c6648910dfad80eebf5ec0fd1a75bfa3ecf114a4d16349d8cf566ac06f4", "raw_generation_error": null, "raw_sealed_at": "2026-08-07T15:42:02Z", "raw_stdout": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    t = int(input_data[0])\n    idx = 1\n    results = []\n    \n    for _ in range(t):\n        n = int(input_data[idx])\n        idx += 1\n        a = [int(x) for x in input_data[idx : idx + n]]\n        idx += n\n        \n        # To maximize the product when adding 1 to exactly one element,\n        # we should add 1 to the smallest element.\n        # This is because (x+1)/x is largest when x is smallest.\n        \n        min_val = min(a)\n        min_idx = a.index(min_val)\n        \n        a[min_idx] += 1\n        \n        product = 1\n        for val in a:\n            product *= val\n        results.append(str(product))\n        \n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\".join(results) + \"\\n\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()\n```", "raw_stdout_sha256": "sha256:558409e2de18136cb7212fa77f1d1ee264f58908de17cef03661f176b033b77c", "refusal": false, "repeat_penalty": 1.05, "row_hash": "sha256:3d2454144687bb91e6de5fdad093896af6efae150e0cbbf0ad56d8faa486d53c", "run_date": "20260807", "runtime": "python_stdio", "schema": "carnot.experiment_6192.live_strategy_seed_stream.v1.raw_row", "seed": 619200015, "selector_features": {"date_bucket": "2023-Q3", "difficulty": "easy", "platform": "codeforces", "prompt_size_bucket": "short", "supported_runtime": "python_stdio", "tag_bucket": "untagged"}, "split": "csl_seed", "stable_task_hash": "sha256:896756a008733de3b97073f2781cb124ca27b15dec5d39eafad67617df433988", "strategy_id": "invariant_first", "strategy_prompt_sha256": "sha256:973248f9221cfafcf6460deacb3340f199948114a56709b5c14712108209d92e", "task_id": "1873_B", "temperature": 0.0, "timeout": false, "timing": {"decode_time_s": 2.549342, "started_monotonic_s": 1213099.757251}, "top_k": 40, "top_p": 1.0, "transport_failure": false, "truncated": false}
+{"cell_id": "sha256:adf9758fa3d5145f42aa140152da2078559af42d32d316563f72ff49620588e8", "chat_messages": [{"content": "You are writing one Python solution for a programming task. Return only raw Python code or one fenced python block. Do not explain the answer.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Seed cell: sha256:adf9758fa3d5145f42aa140152da2078559af42d32d316563f72ff49620588e8\nStrategy: edge_case_guarded\nWrite a Python solution that handles boundary cases first, then the general case. Keep parsing explicit and return only runnable code.\n\nTitle: Loong Tracking\nTakahashi has created a game where the player controls a dragon on a coordinate plane.\nThe dragon consists of N parts numbered 1 to N, with part 1 being called the head.\nInitially, part i is located at the coordinates (i,0). Process Q queries as follows.\n\n- 1 C: Move the head by 1 in direction C. Here, C is one of R, L, U, and D, which represent the positive x-direction, negative x-direction, positive y-direction, and negative y-direction, respectively. Each part other than the head moves to follow the part in front of it. That is, part i (2\\leq i \\leq N) moves to the coordinates where part i-1 was before the move.\n- 2 p: Find the coordinates of part p.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN Q\n\\mathrm{query}_1\n\\vdots\n\\mathrm{query}_Q\n\nEach query is in one of the following two formats:\n1 C\n\n2 p\n\nOutput\n\nPrint q lines, where q is the number of queries of the second type.\nThe i-th line should contain x and y separated by a space, where (x,y) are the answer to the i-th such query.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 10^6\n- 1 \\leq Q \\leq 2\\times 10^5\n- For the first type of query, C is one of R, L, U, and D.\n- For the second type of query, 1\\leq p \\leq N.\n- All numerical input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5 9\n2 3\n1 U\n2 3\n1 R\n1 D\n2 3\n1 L\n2 1\n2 5\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3 0\n2 0\n1 1\n1 0\n1 0\n\nAt each time when processing the second type of query, the parts are at the following positions:\n\nNote that multiple parts may exist at the same coordinates.\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:6bd19cdb706265553f191bcc5c2e8a26802020eb5283bdc10a06096b5e81f8dd", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:c2bf9acdf115fc033678140f02d0922682e4c663aea0591f7a198749fee2bca8", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks us to simulate the movement of a dragon consisting of $N$ parts.\nInitially, part $i$ is at $(i, 0)$ for $1 \\le i \\le N$.\nThere are two types of queries:\n1. `1 C`: Move the head (part 1) by 1 unit in direction $C \\in \\{R, L, U, D\\}$. Then, each part $i$ ($2 \\le i \\le N$) moves to the position where part $i-1$ was *before* this move.\n2. `2 p`: Output the coordinates of part $p$.\n\nConstraints: $N \\le 10^6$, $Q \\le 2 \\times 10^5$.\nA naive simulation would update all $N$ parts for each type 1 query, taking $O(N)$ per query. With $Q$ queries, total time would be $O(NQ)$, which is too slow ($10^{11}$ operations). We need a more efficient approach, likely $O(1)$ or $O(\\log N)$ per query.\n\nLet's trace the movement of parts.\nInitially, part $i$ is at $(i, 0)$.\nWhen part 1 moves, it goes to a new position. Part 2 moves to where part 1 was. Part 3 moves to where part 2 was, and so on.\nThis looks like a queue or a linked list structure. The positions of the parts are essentially shifting along a path traced by the head.\nSpecifically, if we record the sequence of positions visited by the head, say $P_0, P_1, P_2, \\dots$, where $P_0$ is the initial position of the head $(1", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:dc207877f4194d2844485dbdd6d13b13534d9bcc9d149654b5803f55de408676", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "qwen3", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF", "model_name": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cac