+{"cell_id": "sha256:c29ea3222c061f7ec0250534624957e9228e9c892134c2fbd8b566adc3d72aba", "chat_messages": [{"content": "You are writing one Python solution for a programming task. Return only raw Python code or one fenced python block. Do not explain the answer.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Seed cell: sha256:c29ea3222c061f7ec0250534624957e9228e9c892134c2fbd8b566adc3d72aba\nStrategy: invariant_first\nBefore writing code, identify the key invariant or recurrence and then encode it compactly. Return only the final Python code.\n\nTitle: Square Permutation\nYou are given a string S of length N consisting of digits.\nFind the number of square numbers that can be obtained by interpreting a permutation of S as a decimal integer.\nMore formally, solve the following.\nLet s _ i be the number corresponding to the i-th digit (1\\leq i\\leq N) from the beginning of S.\nFind the number of square numbers that can be represented as \\displaystyle \\sum _ {i=1} ^ N s _ {p _ i}10 ^ {N-i} with a permutation P=(p _ 1,p _ 2,\\ldots,p _ N) of (1, \\dots, N).\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\r\nS\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer in a single line.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq 13\n- S is a string of length N consisting of digits.\n- N is an integer.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n4\r\n4320\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2\r\n\nFor P=(4,2,3,1), we have s _ 4\\times10 ^ 3+s _ 2\\times10 ^ 2+s _ 3\\times10 ^ 1+s _ 1=324=18 ^ 2.\r\nFor P=(3,2,4,1), we have s _ 3\\times10 ^ 3+s _ 2\\times10 ^ 2+s _ 4\\times10 ^ 1+s _ 1=2304=48 ^ 2.\nNo other permutations result in square numbers, so you should print 2.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3\r\n010\n\nSample Output 2\n\n2\r\n\nFor P=(1,3,2) or P=(3,1,2), we have \\displaystyle\\sum _ {i=1} ^ Ns _ {p _ i}10 ^ {N-i}=1=1 ^ 2.\r\nFor P=(2,1,3) or P=(2,3,1), we have \\displaystyle\\sum _ {i=1} ^ Ns _ {p _ i}10 ^ {N-i}=100=10 ^ 2.\nNo other permutations result in square numbers, so you should print 2.\r\nNote that different permutations are not distinguished if they result in the same number.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n13\r\n8694027811503\n\nSample Output 3\n\n840\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:1c6a1f564f2ea95b4354f4a01690f6b354e5b0fdf0308921ebb951e5cb5b2821", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:3707cf5240bd773585d3682f1a3e65dd48f0e9fd50ae63835c142b01125a9278", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks us to find the number of distinct square numbers that can be formed by permuting the digits of a given string $S$ of length $N$.\nConstraints: $1 \\le N \\le 13$.\n$S$ consists of digits.\n\nLet's analyze the constraints. $N$ is up to 13. The number of permutations is $N!$, which for $N=13$ is about $6.2 \\times 10^9$. This is too large to iterate over all permutations directly.\nHowever, we are looking for square numbers formed by these digits.\nA square number $X^2$ must have the same multiset of digits as $S$.\nThe maximum value of a number with 13 digits is $10^{13}-1$. So $X^2 < 10^{13}$, which means $X < \\sqrt{10^{13}} \\approx 3.16 \\times 10^6$.\nThe range of possible square roots $X$ is from $1$ to $\\lfloor \\sqrt{10^{13}-1} \\rfloor = 3162277$.\nThis is a manageable range. We can iterate over all possible integers $X$ in this range, compute $Y = X^2$, check if the digits of $Y$ match the multiset of digits in $S$, and count how many distinct such $Y$ exist.\n\nWait, the problem says \"Find the number of square numbers that can be obtained...\". It also says \"Note that different permutations are not distinguished if they result in the same number.\" This means we just need to count the number of distinct values $Y$ such that $Y$ is a perfect square and the digits of $Y", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:93f4ded86055e2682fdcc142292871820829169410712da3f7efbbc6d5649836", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "qwen3", "model_hf_id": "unslo