c}$ using at most one operation.\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:15ccf367b5bae2b63068ee055a6f18de1d2da3529c0a38c6c8643767bffd5f25", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:8b3cbb577faed40abaa11b752ab48562ec2241ac80152f07d081f386021aa3e2", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Read all input lines and filter out empty ones\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    t = int(input_data[0])\n    test_cases = input_data[1:]\n    \n    target = \"abc\"\n    \n    for i in range(t):\n        s = test_cases[i]\n        \n        # Boundary Case: Already sorted (0 swaps needed)\n        if s == target:\n            print(\"YES\")\n            continue\n            \n        # General Case: Check if exactly one swap can result in \"abc\"\n        # A single swap of two elements in a 3-element string results in \n        # exactly one element being in its correct position, or all three.\n        # Since we already checked s == target, we check if exactly one \n        # character is in the correct position.\n        \n        correct_positions = 0\n        for char_idx in range(3):\n            if s[char_idx] == target[char_idx]:\n                correct_positions += 1\n        \n        # If 1 character is in the correct place, the other two must be \n        # swapped (e.g., 'acb' -> 'a' is correct, 'c' and 'b' are swapped).\n        # If 0 characters are in the correct place, it requires more than one swap (e.g., 'bca').\n        # If 2 characters are in the correct place, that's impossible for a permutation of abc.\n        if correct_positions == 1:\n            print(\"YES\")\n        else:\n            print", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:c1ccb79b8267625aac248ca160903b9e593a8bbe20f4f838503ee5b7792f3ce2", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_fam