ks like a greedy problem. We scan the string from left to right. When we encounter a 'B', we must cover it. To minimize the total number of operations, when we find the first 'B' at index $i$, we should apply an operation that covers this 'B'. Which operation is best?\nSince we are scanning from left to right, any 'B' at index $j < i$ has already been handled (turned to 'W'). The current 'B' at $i$ needs to be covered. We can choose a range $[x, x+k-1]$ such that $x \\le i \\le x+k-1$.\nTo help with future 'B's (which will be", "raw_stdout_sha256": "sha256:c0bb89796ab3bd2a94cbfc34ce080e72a8698f4f0c8c5c7985ce488ca4711a18", "refusal": false, "repeat_penalty": 1.05, "row_hash": "sha256:441f6514e28c2ed99dfed60adc74c09e4123b81f9659f5194451dabaa2853b82", "run_date": "20260807", "runtime": "python_stdio", "schema": "carnot.experiment_6192.live_strategy_seed_stream.v1.raw_row", "seed": 619200042, "selector_features": {"date_bucket": "2023-Q3", "difficulty": "easy", "platform": "codeforces", "prompt_size_bucket": "medium", "supported_runtime": "python_stdio", "tag_bucket": "untagged"}, "split": "csl_seed", "stable_task_hash": "sha256:930541d546d4dabbc4be1ebb85b9bf6fdcb526cb9687767e9d73dcd20a909e5c", "strategy_id": "direct_implementation", "strategy_prompt_sha256": "sha256:0936e7f99b76be2579a31a16fbd66f368cbe79e1ce12b726f67bd7f08213bf2f", "task_id": "1873_D", "temperature": 0.0, "timeout": false, "timing": {"decode_time_s": 3.381653, "started_monotonic_s": 1212933.734202}, "top_k": 40, "top_p": 1.0, "transport_failure": false, "truncated": true}
