ken_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:50c438656c474c58ce290ce6ee068dd226462b09efcd7b289551e606b5a8f29f", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks us to determine if any two chords intersect on a circle.\nThere are $2N$ points on a circle, numbered 1 to $2N$ clockwise.\nWe are given $N$ chords, each connecting two distinct points. All $2N$ endpoints are distinct.\nTwo chords $(A_i, B_i)$ and $(A_j, B_j)$ intersect if and only if their endpoints alternate around the circle.\nThat is, if we traverse the circle clockwise starting from some point, we encounter the endpoints in an order like $A_i, A_j, B_i, B_j$ or $A_i, B_j, B_i, A_j$, etc.\nMore formally, let's normalize each chord so that $L_i = \\min(A_i, B_i)$ and $R_i = \\max(A_i, B_i)$.\nTwo chords $(L_i, R_i)$ and $(L_j, R_j)$ intersect if and only if $L_i < L_j < R_i < R_j$ or $L_j < L_i < R_j < R_i$.\nThis is a classic problem that can be solved using a stack or by checking intervals.\nSince all endpoints are distinct and cover $1$ to $2N$, we can process the points in order from 1 to $2N$.\nWhen we encounter the first endpoint of a chord, we push it onto a stack.\nWhen we encounter the second endpoint of a chord, we check if it matches the top of the stack.\nIf it does, we pop the stack. This means the chord is properly nested or disjoint from others processed so far without crossing.\nIf it doesn't match the top of the stack, it means this chord crosses some previously opened chord that hasn't been closed yet. In this case, an intersection exists.\nWait", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:a4fd20e0f716539ccb26780e81316c27220e546bd3176fa79154fd130ea29a05", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "qwen3", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF", "model_name": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF/snapshots/a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf", "model_quantization": "UD-Q4_K_M", "model_revision": "a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c", "n_ctx": 4096, "order_index": 43, "private_test_sha256": "sha256:3e245b8fdf040942cbb7da974c2fd33dc800ff039aa3d57392fab6ef467c240c", "prompt_sha256": "sha256:da50ccb44d98f93b1dcfabfc860cd31db4b471e4109e39a272e9c9ebbf55636f", "prompt_token_count": 508, "public_test_sha256": "sha256:3d8d9273c43827c00272c13add3fc50e9a0b9e1dfdc37916545eac2bbd552772", "raw_generation_error": null, "raw_sealed_at": "2026-08-07T15:39:03Z", "raw_stdout": "The problem asks us to determine if any two chords intersect on a circle.\nThere are $2N$ points on a circle, numbered 1 to $2N$ clockwise.\nWe are given $N$ chords, each connecting two distinct points. All $2N$ endpoints are distinct.\nTwo chords $(A_i, B_i)$ and $(A_j, B_j)$ intersect if and only if their endpoints alternate around the circle.\nThat is, if we traverse the circle clockwise starting from some point, we encounter the endpoints in an order like $A_i, A_j, B_i, B_j$ or $A_i, B_j, B_i, A_j$, etc.\nMore formally, let's normalize each chord so that $L_i = \\min(A_i, B_i)$ and $R_i = \\max(A_i, B_i)$.\nTwo chords $(L_i, R_i)$ and $(L_j, R_j)$ intersect if and only if $L_i < L_j < R_i < R_j$ or $L_j < L_i < R_j < R_i$.\nThis is a classic problem that can be solved using a stack or by checking intervals.\nSince all endpoints are distinct and cover $1$ to $2N$, we can process the points in order from 1 to $2N$.\nWhen we encounter the first endpoint of a chord, we push it onto a stack.\nWhen we encounter the second endpoint of a chord, we check if it matches the top of the stack.\nIf it does, we pop the stack. This means the chord is properly nested or disjoint from others processed so far without crossing.\nIf it doesn't match the top of the stack, it means this chord crosses some previously opened chord that hasn't been closed yet. In this case, an intersection exists.\nWait", "raw_stdout_sha256": "sha256:a4fd20e0f716539ccb26780e81316c27220e546bd3176f