, so you should print 2.\r\nNote that different permutations are not distinguished if they result in the same number.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n13\r\n8694027811503\n\nSample Output 3\n\n840\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:91ae6194ea1d0bcb0baae436c12d91808f8771387121458226cfb78315aebb34", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:32f63b5067628b7317ee52ce0c44ab1e1433e9bdd7143e29c865d2b987c26cbf", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    try:\n        line1 = sys.stdin.readline()\n        if not line1:\n            return\n        n = int(line1.strip())\n        s_str = sys.stdin.readline().strip()\n    except EOFError:\n        return\n\n    # Count frequency of each digit in the input string\n    target_counts = [0] * 10\n    for char in s_str:\n        target_counts[int(char)] += 1\n    \n    # The maximum possible number is 10^N - 1\n    # Since N <= 13, max value is 9,999,999,999,999\n    # Max square root is sqrt(10^13) approx 3,162,277\n    \n    limit = 10**n\n    max_sqrt = int((limit - 1)**0.5)\n    \n    ans_set = set()\n    \n    # Iterate through all possible square numbers up to the limit\n    # A number is a valid permutation if its digit counts match target_counts\n    for i in range(max_sqrt + 1):\n        square = i * i\n        s_sq = str(square)\n        \n        # The problem states we interpret a permutation of S as a decimal integer.\n        # This means the resulting number must have exactly N digits when considering\n        # leading zeros (as per Sample 2: \"010\" can form 1 and 100).\n        # Effectively, we need to check if the digit counts of 'square' padded with \n        # leading zeros to length N match the target_", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:0b3c3c1f8c00a04afcb8201345d11bfc7970578829d7801230d19fd787c1b009", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "gemma4", "m