+{"cell_id": "sha256:ab2ab0a86f7fec627893e0d0942b5bb12be4a4d8e92c500a6d61da3989561d2d", "chat_messages": [{"content": "You are writing one Python solution for a programming task. Return only raw Python code or one fenced python block. Do not explain the answer.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Seed cell: sha256:ab2ab0a86f7fec627893e0d0942b5bb12be4a4d8e92c500a6d61da3989561d2d\nStrategy: direct_implementation\nImplement the requested Python solution directly. Prefer clear control flow, standard input/output handling when no starter function is provided, and return only runnable code.\n\nTitle: A. Short Sort\nThere are three cards with letters $\\texttt{a}$, $\\texttt{b}$, $\\texttt{c}$ placed in a row in some order. You can do the following operation at most once: \n\n \n-  Pick two cards, and swap them.  Is it possible that the row becomes $\\texttt{abc}$ after the operation? Output \"YES\" if it is possible, and \"NO\" otherwise.\n\nInput\n\nThe first line contains a single integer $t$ ($1 \\leq t \\leq 6$)\u00a0\u2014 the number of test cases.\n\nThe only line of each test case contains a single string consisting of each of the three characters $\\texttt{a}$, $\\texttt{b}$, and $\\texttt{c}$ exactly once, representing the cards.\n\nOutput\n\nFor each test case, output \"YES\" if you can make the row $\\texttt{abc}$ with at most one operation, or \"NO\" otherwise.\n\nYou can output the answer in any case (for example, the strings \"yEs\", \"yes\", \"Yes\" and \"YES\" will be recognized as a positive answer).Sample Input 1:\n6\n\nabc\n\nacb\n\nbac\n\nbca\n\ncab\n\ncba\n\n\n\nSample Output 1:\n\nYES\nYES\nYES\nNO\nNO\nYES\n\n\nNote\n\nIn the first test case, we don't need to do any operations, since the row is already $\\texttt{abc}$.\n\nIn the second test case, we can swap $\\texttt{c}$ and $\\texttt{b}$: $\\texttt{acb} \\to \\texttt{abc}$.\n\nIn the third test case, we can swap $\\texttt{b}$ and $\\texttt{a}$: $\\texttt{bac} \\to \\texttt{abc}$.\n\nIn the fourth test case, it is impossible to make $\\texttt{abc}$ using at most one operation.\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:6506c4726f61fff06388df28551d90478b418409e55088af5cceaf81407f1ac5", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:944c19abdfbc9acb390cab313833ee6d4ac638d9ac27e612888d04c83a8273f7", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks whether a given permutation of the string \"abc\" can be transformed into \"abc\" by performing at most one swap operation.\nThere are 3! = 6 possible permutations of \"abc\".\nLet's list them and see which ones require 0 or 1 swap to become \"abc\".\nTarget: \"abc\"\n\n1. \"abc\": Already \"abc\". 0 swaps needed. -> YES\n2. \"acb\": Swap 'c' and 'b' (indices 1 and 2) -> \"abc\". 1 swap needed. -> YES\n3. \"bac\": Swap 'b' and 'a' (indices 0 and 1) -> \"abc\". 1 swap needed. -> YES\n4. \"bca\": \n   - Swap indices 0,1 -> \"cba\" (not abc)\n   - Swap indices 0,2 -> \"acb\" (not abc)\n   - Swap indices 1,2 -> \"bac\" (not abc)\n   Wait, let's check carefully.\n   \"bca\" -> swap(0,1) -> \"cba\"\n   \"bca\" -> swap(0,2) -> \"acb\"\n   \"bca\" -> swap(1,2) -> \"bac\"\n   None of these are \"abc\". So \"bca\" requires more than 1 swap. Actually, \"bca\" is a cyclic shift. It takes 2 swaps to become \"abc\". -> NO\n5. \"cab\":\n   - Swap(0,1) -> \"acb\"\n   - Swap(0,2) -> \"bac\"\n   - Swap(1,2) -> \"cba\"\n   None are \"abc\". -> NO\n6. \"cba\": Swap(0,2) -> \"abc", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:be942cec87984cbd2e8ae9b0ee93bede737b170a2d390e9d507f1c1dfff81109", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "qwen3", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF", "model_name": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF/snapshots/a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf", "model_quantization": "UD-Q4_K_M", "model