sible, it makes sense to extend the interval as far to the right as possible.\nSo, if we decide to cover $p_1$, the optimal choice is to place the interval starting at $p_1$ (if $p_1 \\ge k-1$) or starting at 0? Wait, the interval has length $k$. If it covers index $i$, it can be $[i-k+1, i]$, $[i-k+2, i+1]$, ..., $[i, i+k-1]$.\nActually, the operation is \"take any $k$ consecutive cells\". So an interval is", "raw_stdout_sha256": "sha256:4e2dcb126f6fe1c2279a0ff0d7bc5fdb210f681b642311f1cf2b2e66e7ba23d6", "refusal": false, "repeat_penalty": 1.05, "row_hash": "sha256:3f49235d636ac4e578d6e407d65a8fd53f5e115765173cb61a19547a83bf38b7", "run_date": "20260807", "runtime": "python_stdio", "schema": "carnot.experiment_6192.live_strategy_seed_stream.v1.raw_row", "seed": 619200078, "selector_features": {"date_bucket": "2023-Q3", "difficulty": "easy", "platform": "codeforces", "prompt_size_bucket": "medium", "supported_runtime": "python_stdio", "tag_bucket": "untagged"}, "split": "csl_seed", "stable_task_hash": "sha256:930541d546d4dabbc4be1ebb85b9bf6fdcb526cb9687767e9d73dcd20a909e5c", "strategy_id": "invariant_first", "strategy_prompt_sha256": "sha256:973248f9221cfafcf6460deacb3340f199948114a56709b5c14712108209d92e", "task_id": "1873_D", "temperature": 0.0, "timeout": false, "timing": {"decode_time_s": 3.202618, "started_monotonic_s": 1212996.500406}, "top_k": 40, "top_p": 1.0, "transport_failure": false, "truncated": true}
+{"cell_id": "sha256:70d2e3ccafccad21c65204ab4a92c796dbf7c38a6526e0ba3e31e291382c5b84", "chat_messages": [{"content": "You are writing one Python solution for a programming task. Return only raw Python code or one fenced python block. Do not explain the answer.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Seed cell: sha256:70d2e3ccafccad21c65204ab4a92c796dbf7c38a6526e0ba3e31e291382c5b84\nStrategy: invariant_first\nBefore writing code, identify the key invariant or recurrence and then encode it compactly. Return only the final Python code.\n\nTitle: Loong Tracking\nTakahashi has created a game where the player controls a dragon on a coordinate plane.\nThe dragon consists of N parts numbered 1 to N, with part 1 being called the head.\nInitially, part i is located at the coordinates (i,0). Process Q queries as follows.\n\n- 1 C: Move the head by 1 in direction C. Here, C is one of R, L, U, and D, which represent the positive x-direction, negative x-direction, positive y-direction, and negative y-direction, respectively. Each part other than the head moves to follow the part in front of it. That is, part i (2\\leq i \\leq N) moves to the coordinates where part i-1 was before the move.\n- 2 p: Find the coordinates of part p.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN Q\n\\mathrm{query}_1\n\\vdots\n\\mathrm{query}_Q\n\nEach query is in one of the following two formats:\n1 C\n\n2 p\n\nOutput\n\nPrint q lines, where q is the number of queries of the second type.\nThe i-th line should contain x and y separated by a space, where (x,y) are the answer to the i-th such query.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 10^6\n- 1 \\leq Q \\leq 2\\times 10^5\n- For the first type of query, C is one of R, L, U, and D.\n- For the second type of query, 1\\leq p \\leq N.\n- All numerical input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n5 9\n2 3\n1 U\n2 3\n1 R\n1 D\n2 3\n1 L\n2 1\n2 5\n\nSample Output 1\n\n3 0\n2 0\n1 1\n1 0\n1 0\n\nAt each time when processing the second type of query, the parts are at the following positions:\n\nNote that multiple parts may exist at the same coordinates.\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:baabb3944899e6485d6c41f8966a662a630db664ec28f671ac3d7e0f588b5f6e", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:be6241e8bbef9fa1576deca78f15b3a9ce23d1427e167e1dad985d985d0782d4", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    Q = int(input_data[1])\n    \n    # The position of part p at time t is the position of the head at time (t - (p-1)).\n    # We store the history of the head's positions.\n    # Initial positions: part i is at (i, 0).\n    # This means at t=0, part 1 is at (1,0), part 2 is at (2,0), ..., part N is at (N,0).\n    # However, the rule says \"part i moves to where part i-1 was before the move\".\n    # Let H(t) be the position of the head at time t.\n    # Let P(i, t) be the position of part i at time t.\n    # P(1, t) = H(t)\n    # P(i, t) = P(i-1, t-1) for i > 1.\n    # By induction: P(i, t) = P(1, t - (i-1)) = H(t - i + 1).\n    # For t < i-1, we need to define H(negative index).\n    # At t=0, P(i, 0) = (i, 0).\n    # Let's trace:\n    # t=0: P(1,0)=(1,0), P(2,0)=(2,0), ..., P(N,0)=(N,0)\n    #", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:2acb3021afdb70e739cd8d377e853a63aeeb97eba7a4fca00fbba9de8d41d8e5", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "gemma4", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-GGUF", "model_name": "Gemma4-26B-A4B-it", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-GGUF/snapshots/3365c68df1a83799b846d05324ebfadbb8cc70b3/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf", "model_quantization": "UD-Q4_K_M", "model_revision": "3365c68df1a83799b846d05324ebfadbb8cc70b3", "n_ctx": 4096, "order_index": 79, "private_test_sha256": "sha256:74e24d334b9ef7104d6be51241841823a86cf7b33daac295a13250f74ef8c31e", "prompt_sha256": "sha256:2166aa3b9cce1154bf1f1355316bdca38832335f3fb69200e4826789eb452c28", "prompt_token_count": 609, "public_test_sha256": "sha256:231b28e38cf0d10c9dc6e6f998c37016df7c9a4b3bf8039ea7fec69995b72bb2", "raw_generation_error": null, "raw_sealed_at": "2026-08-07T15:42:02Z", "raw_stdout": "```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    Q = int(input_data[1])\n    \n    # The position of part p at time t is the position of the head at time (t - (p-1)).\n    # We store the history of the head's positions.\n    # Initial positions: part i is at (i, 0).\n    # This means at t=0, part 1 is at (1,0), part 2 is at (2,0), ..., part N is at (N,0).\n    # However, the rule says \"part i moves to where part i-1 was before the move\".\n    # Let H(t) be the position of the head at time t.\n    # Let P(i, t) be the position of part i at time t.\n    # P(1, t) = H(t)\n    # P(i, t) = P(i-1, t-1) for i > 1.\n    # By induction: P(i, t) = P(1, t - (i-1)) = H(t - i + 1).\n    # For t < i-1, we need to define H(negative index).\n    # At t=0, P(i, 0) = (i, 0).\n    # Let's trace:\n    # t=0: P(1,0)=(1,0), P(2,0)=(2,0), ..., P(N,0)=(N,0)\n    #", "raw_stdout_sha256": "sha256:2acb3021afdb70e739cd8d377e853a63aeeb97eba7a4fca00fbba9de8d41d8e5", "refusal": false, "repeat_penalty": 1.05, "row_hash": "sha256:95e29e3c8cbaefbd217879a4e0f530cf4420434a96aa078debc997d0048edc38", "run_date": "20260807", "runtime": "python_stdio", "schema": "carnot.experiment_6192.live_strategy_seed_stream.v1.raw_row", "seed": 619200079, "selector_features": {"date_bucket": "2024-Q1", "difficulty": "medium", "platform": "atcoder", "prompt_size_bucket": "short", "supported_runtime": "python_stdio", "tag_bucket": "untagged"}, "split": "csl_seed", "stable_task_hash": "sha256:bbf447f97098462cae539c735d616233fdb228efebebfd911da4235abcf3cdeb", "strategy_id": "invariant_first", "strategy_prompt_sha256": "sha256:973248f9221cfafcf6460deacb3340f199948114a56709b5c14712108209d92e", "task_id": "abc335_c", "temperature": 0.0, "timeout": false, "timing": {"decode_time_s": 3.763107, "started_monotonic_s": 1213202.463348}, "top_k": 40, "top_p": 1.0, "transport_failure": false, "truncated": true}
+{"cell_id": "sha256:d2e4bf1e801e4390c1886d927e2658a9e2788d0acd48fbb948878557c9c5af55", "chat_messages": [{"content": "You are writing one Python solution for a programming task. Return only raw Python code or one fenced python block. Do not explain the answer.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Seed cell: sha256:d2e4bf1e801e4390c1886d927e2658a9e2788d0acd48fbb948878557c9c5af55\nStrategy: edge_case_guarded\nWrite a Python solution that handles boundary cases first, then the general case. Keep parsing explicit and return only runnable code.\n\nTitle: Online Shopping\nAtCoder Inc. sells merchandise through its online shop.\nTakahashi has decided to purchase N types of products from there.\nFor each integer i from 1 to N, the i-th type of product has a price of P_i yen each, and he will buy Q_i of this.\nAdditionally, he must pay a shipping fee.\nThe shipping fee is 0 yen if the total price of the products purchased is S yen or above, and K yen otherwise.\nHe will pay the total price of the products purchased plus the shipping fee.\nCalculate the amount he will pay.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN S K\nP_1 Q_1\nP_2 Q_2\n\\vdots\nP_N Q_N\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the amount Takahashi will pay for online shopping.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq N\\leq 100\n- 1\\leq S\\leq 10000\n- 1\\leq K\\leq 10000\n- 1\\leq P_i\\leq 10000\n- 1\\leq Q_i\\leq 100\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n2 2000 500\n1000 1\n100 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2100\n\nTakahashi buys one product for 1000 yen and six products for 100 yen each.\nThus, the total price of the products is 1000\\times 1+100\\times 6=1600 yen.\nSince the total amount for the products is less than 2000 yen, the shipping fee will be 500 yen.\nTherefore, the amount Takahashi will pay is 1600+500=2100 yen.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 2000 500\n1000 1\n100 6\n5000 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n6600\n\nThe total price of the products is 1000\\times 1+100\\times 6+5000\\times 1=6600 yen.\nSince the total amount for the products is not less than 2000 yen, the shipping fee will be 0 yen.\nTherefore, the amount Takahashi will pay is 6600+0=6600 yen.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n2 2000 500\n1000 1\n1000 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\n2000\n\nThere may be multiple products with the same price per item.\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:9a9e47ded08174cdaf96a691560cd194d31644a47d5f14d60e7aa2b5a6cc1ea5", "code_extraction": {"method": "fenced_code_block", "status": "ok"}, "completion_token_count": 299, "content_hash": "sha256:54c21999f0a03732e8407cb07a02bc002f8c4bed16490708cb662cf2b1ad4db1", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "import sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Explicitly parse the first line of input\n    input_data = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input_data:\n        return\n    \n    N = int(input_data[0])\n    S = int(input_data[1])\n    K = int(input_data[2])\n    \n    products = []\n    idx = 3\n    for _ in range(N):\n        p = int(input_data[idx])\n        q = int(input_data[idx+1])\n        products.append((p, q))\n        idx += 2\n        \n    # Calculate total product price\n    total_product_price = 0\n    for p, q in products:\n        total_product_price += p * q\n        \n    # Boundary case/Logic check: Determine shipping fee\n    # If total price is S or above, shipping is 0. Otherwise, it's K.\n    if total_product_price >= S:\n        shipping_fee = 0\n    else:\n        shipping_fee = K\n        \n    # Final amount calculation\n    total_amount = total_product_price + shipping_fee\n    print(total_amount)\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    solve()", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:c2ad6047baa59777036034d459130b1f51a44a3fb8aa0108a6863001468b4585", "finish_reason": "stop", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "gemma4", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-GGUF", "model_name": "Gemma4-26B-A4B-it", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-GGUF/snapshots/3365c68df1