af111", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:f1d9fef307ff518eeb24a3781ab2910bee087b454a0053ad68c3eec1871faff9", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks us to sort $N$ people based on their success rates, which are defined as $\\frac{A_i}{A_i+B_i}$.\nThe sorting criteria are:\n1. Descending order of success rate.\n2. If success rates are equal, ascending order of person index (1-based).\n\nInput format:\nFirst line: $N$\nNext $N$ lines: $A_i \\ B_i$ for each person $i=1 \\dots N$.\n\nConstraints:\n$N \\le 2 \\times 10^5$\n$A_i, B_i \\le 10^9$\n$A_i + B_i \\ge 1$\n\nLet's denote the success rate of person $i$ as $R_i = \\frac{A_i}{A_i+B_i}$.\nWe need to sort indices $1, \\dots, N$ such that if $i$ comes before $j$, then either $R_i > R_j$ or ($R_i = R_j$ and $i < j$).\n\nComparing two fractions $\\frac{a}{b}$ and $\\frac{c}{d}$ where $b, d > 0$:\n$\\frac{a}{b} > \\frac{c}{d} \\iff a \\cdot d > c \\cdot b$.\nHere, for person $i$, numerator is $A_i$ and denominator is $A_i+B_i$.\nFor person $j$, numerator is $A_j$ and denominator is $A_j+B_j$.\nSo, $R_i > R_j \\iff A_i(A_j+B_j) > A_j(A_i+B_i)$.\nThis comparison involves multiplication of numbers up to $10^9 \\times 2 \\times 10^9 = 2 \\times ", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:b073cb28c1ea49d273a60527a008da427c5be5f38bb6c1b4c62903ff9adb25af", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "qwen3", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF", "model_name": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF/snapshots/a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf", "model_quantization": "UD-Q4_K_M", "model_revision": "a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c", "n_ctx": 4096, "order_index": 87, "private_test_sha256": "sha256:89afb067efa5e89cbcee7ee4c5ae94086d12788d7135cdc4e8ffc261f0992b81", "prompt_sha256": "sha256