+{"cell_id": "sha256:a3dd5e3a571cb46b8b374cba2abb349256fa740216cce0407d8bdff28eb3e627", "chat_messages": [{"content": "You are writing one Python solution for a programming task. Return only raw Python code or one fenced python block. Do not explain the answer.", "role": "system"}, {"content": "Seed cell: sha256:a3dd5e3a571cb46b8b374cba2abb349256fa740216cce0407d8bdff28eb3e627\nStrategy: edge_case_guarded\nWrite a Python solution that handles boundary cases first, then the general case. Keep parsing explicit and return only runnable code.\n\nTitle: Chords\nThere are 2N points placed at equal intervals on a circle, numbered 1 to 2N in a clockwise direction starting from a certain point.\nThere are also N chords on the circle, with the i-th chord connecting points A_i and B_i.\nIt is guaranteed that all the values A_1,\\dots,A_N,B_1,\\dots,B_N are distinct.\nDetermine whether there is an intersection between the chords.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN\nA_1 B_1\nA_2 B_2\n\\vdots\nA_N B_N\n\nOutput\n\nIf there is an intersection between the chords, print Yes; otherwise, print No.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2\\leq N \\leq 2\\times 10^5\n- 1\\leq A_i,B_i \\leq 2N\n- A_1,\\dots,A_N,B_1,\\dots,B_N are all distinct\n- All input values are integers\n\nSample Input 1\n\n3\n1 3\n4 2\n5 6\n\nSample Output 1\n\nYes\n\n\nAs shown in the figure, chord 1 (the line segment connecting points 1 and 3) and chord 2 (the line segment connecting points 4 and 2) intersect, so print Yes.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3\n6 1\n4 3\n2 5\n\nSample Output 2\n\nNo\n\n\nAs shown in the figure, there is no intersection between the chords, so print No.\n\nSample Input 3\n\n4\n2 4\n3 7\n8 6\n5 1\n\nSample Output 3\n\nYes\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:d43831bd58d4d493830db2a5b0e197bf74c40f65058bbd2c9abe5026cf8206a4", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:b50cb4a4314a47687183ad4aa7c3d62d4e6fc28f4dfb28fc9e8eed106bec3745", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks us to determine if any two chords intersect on a circle.\nThere are $2N$ points on a circle, numbered 1 to $2N$ clockwise.\nWe are given $N$ chords, each connecting two distinct points. All $2N$ endpoints are distinct.\nTwo chords $(A, B)$ and $(C, D)$ intersect if and only if their endpoints alternate around the circle. That is, if we traverse the circle from 1 to $2N$, we encounter the endpoints in an order like $A, C, B, D$ or $A, D, B, C$.\nMore formally, let's normalize each chord so that $A_i < B_i$. Then two chords $(A_1, B_1)$ and $(A_2, B_2)$ intersect if and only if $A_1 < A_2 < B_1 < B_2$ or $A_2 < A_1 < B_2 < B_1$.\nWait, this condition holds if we consider the points on a line from 1 to $2N$. But since it's a circle, does the \"wrap-around\" matter?\nActually, the standard condition for chords on a circle intersecting is exactly that their endpoints interleave. If we map the circle to a line by cutting it at some point not occupied by an endpoint (e.g., between $2N$ and 1), the interleaving condition remains valid. Since all points are distinct and cover $1 \\dots 2N$, any two chords will intersect iff their endpoints alternate.\nLet's verify this. Suppose we have chords $(1, 3)$ and $(2, 4)$. Endpoints: 1, 2, 3, 4. Order: 1, 2, 3, 4", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:a57c19320d398d7631915d6ea781fb95f475b4bb2613ec274a50272398b67f89", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "qwen3", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF", "model_name": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF/snapshots/a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf", "model_quantization": "UD-Q4_K_M", "model_revision": "a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c", "n_ctx": 4096, "order_index": 95, "private_test_sha256": "sha256:3e245b8fdf040942cbb7da974c2fd33dc800ff039aa3d57392fab6ef467c240c", "prompt_sha256": "sha256:da50ccb44d98f93b1dcfabfc860cd31db4b471e4109e39a272e9c9ebbf55636f", "prompt_token_count": 501, "public_test_sha256": "sha256:3d8d9273c43827c00272c13add3fc50e9a0b9e1dfdc37916545eac2bbd552772", "raw_generation_error": null, "raw_sealed_at": "2026-08-07T15:39:03Z", "raw_stdout": "The problem asks us to determine if any two chords intersect on a circle.\nThere are $2N$ points on a circle, numbered 1 to $2N$ clockwise.\nWe are given $N$ chords, each connecting two distinct points. All $2N$ endpoints are distinct.\nTwo chords $(A, B)$ and $(C, D)$ intersect if and only if their endpoints alternate around the circle. That is, if we traverse the circle from 1 to $2N$, we encounter the endpoints in an order like $A, C, B, D$ or $A, D, B, C$.\nMore formally, let's normalize each chord so that $A_i < B_i$. Then two chords $(A_1, B_1)$ and $(A_2, B_2)$ intersect if and only if $A_1 < A_2 < B_1 < B_2$ or $A_2 < A_1 < B_2 < B_1$.\nWait, this condition holds if we consider the points on a line from 1 to $2N$. But since it's a circle, does the \"wrap-around\" matter?\nActually, the standard condition for chords on a circle intersecting is exactly that their endpoints interleave. If we map the circle to a line by cutting it at some point not occupied by an endpoint (e.g., between $2N$ and 1), the interleaving condition remains valid. Since all points are distinct and cover $1 \\dots 2N$, any two chords will intersect iff their endpoints alternate.\nLet's verify this. Suppose we have chords $(1, 3)$ and $(2, 4)$. Endpoints: 1, 2, 3, 4. Order: 1, 2, 3, 4", "raw_stdout_sha256": "sha256:a57c19320d398d7631915d6ea781fb95f475b4bb2613ec274a50272398b67f89", "refusal": false, "repeat_penalty": 1.05, "row_hash": "sha256:191a9ced62f96994059cc5bc3acd542940eb455edf08673654dafc224353da23", "run_date": "20260807", "runtime": "python_stdio", "schema": "carnot.experiment_6192.live_strategy_seed_stream.v1.raw_row", "seed": 61920009