cond type of query, the parts are at the following positions:\n\nNote that multiple parts may exist at the same coordinates.\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:5ddb5bfd37744cec389b496d5bb2057ac8b5272ad531965984ceca94ac15511c", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:86e57f49c409ff3cbe8508c0a4976baefe26526a753aea77be2e1368d6c1c562", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks us to simulate the movement of a dragon consisting of $N$ parts.\nInitially, part $i$ is at $(i, 0)$ for $1 \\le i \\le N$.\nThere are two types of queries:\n1. `1 C`: Move the head (part 1) by 1 unit in direction $C \\in \\{R, L, U, D\\}$. Then, each part $i$ ($2 \\le i \\le N$) moves to the position where part $i-1$ was *before* this move.\n2. `2 p`: Output the current coordinates of part $p$.\n\nLet's trace the movement carefully.\nInitially:\nPart 1: $(1, 0)$\nPart 2: $(2, 0)$\n...\nPart $N$: $(N, 0)$\n\nQuery `1 C`:\nLet the position of part $i$ before the move be $P_i$.\nThe head moves to a new position $P'_1$. The direction $C$ determines $P'_1$.\nFor $i \\ge 2$, part $i$ moves to where part $i-1$ was. So, $P'_i = P_{i-1}$.\nThis means the positions shift: $P'_2 = P_1$, $P'_3 = P_2$, ..., $P'_N = P_{N-1}$.\nThe new position of the head is $P'_1$.\n\nWait, let's re-read carefully: \"Each part other than the head moves to follow the part in front of it. That is, part i (2\\leq i \\leq N) moves to the coordinates where part i-1 was before the move.\"\nYes, this means $P", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:856f4ab64a45aac6d28a410cab500ccf62c637ae4cc6396d563105c129af598b", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "qwen3", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF", "model_name": "Qwen3.6-35B-A3B", "model_path": "/home/ianblenke/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--unsloth--Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF/snapshots/a483e9e6cbd595906af30beda3187c2663a1118c/Qwen3.6-35B-A