 0\n1 1\n1 0\n1 0\n\nAt each time when processing the second type of query, the parts are at the following positions:\n\nNote that multiple parts may exist at the same coordinates.\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:1281b7612c0201ef0bf3d3ba2da37b9318bd181f8211c01771ebd9c349465c82", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:0135c043a5c79cf52bc10a906fe9c7690c5f26ddea1aca2823e935756bd8f236", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks us to simulate the movement of a dragon consisting of $N$ parts.\nInitially, part $i$ is at $(i, 0)$ for $1 \\le i \\le N$.\nThere are two types of queries:\n1. `1 C`: Move the head (part 1) by 1 unit in direction $C \\in \\{R, L, U, D\\}$. Then, each part $i$ ($2 \\le i \\le N$) moves to the position where part $i-1$ was *before* this move.\n2. `2 p`: Output the coordinates of part $p$.\n\nConstraints: $N \\le 10^6$, $Q \\le 2 \\times 10^5$.\nA naive simulation would update all $N$ parts for each type 1 query, taking $O(N)$ per query. With $Q$ queries, total time would be $O(NQ)$, which is too slow ($10^{11}$ operations). We need a more efficient approach, likely $O(1)$ or $O(\\log N)$ per query.\n\nLet's trace the movement of a single part.\nInitially, part $i$ is at $(i, 0)$.\nWhen the head moves, it changes its position. The other parts follow the previous positions of the parts in front of them.\nThis means the position of part $p$ at time $t$ depends on the history of movements of the head and the initial positions.\nSpecifically, if we denote the position of part $i$ at time $t$ as $P_i(t)$, then:\n$P_1(t) = P_1(t-1) + \\Delta_t$, where $\\Delta_t$ is the move vector for query", "extracted_code_sha256": "sha256:e54f045812ebc0ade10e483eac2a727455970c1df4761a55343313f3a7867cce", "finish_reason": "length", "max_tokens": 384, "model_family": "qwen3", "model_hf_id": "unsloth/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF", "model_name": "Qwen3.