olor{red}{\\texttt{BWB}}\\texttt{WW} \\to \\texttt{WWWWWWW}$$\n\nIn the third test case you can perform the following operations: $$\\texttt{B}\\color{red}{\\texttt{WBWB}} \\to \\color{red}{\\texttt{BWWW}}\\texttt{W} \\to \\texttt{WWWWW}$$\n", "role": "user"}], "chat_messages_sha256": "sha256:d9de5d0e14752a0636afedcc884d2b55ed87af852224ec93926402fca8fa4fbe", "code_extraction": {"method": "raw_stdout", "status": "no_code_block"}, "completion_token_count": 384, "content_hash": "sha256:e6ecb70855167cf5bea613b548fa59427615a75490460774e999092a49a30042", "entry_point": "solve", "extracted_code": "The problem asks for the minimum number of operations to remove all black cells ('B') from a string of length $n$. An operation consists of choosing any $k$ consecutive cells and turning them all white ('W').\nWe are given $t$ test cases. For each test case, we get $n$, $k$, and the string $s$.\nConstraints: $1 \\le k \\le n \\le 2 \\cdot 10^5$, sum of $n$ over test cases $\\le 2 \\cdot 10^5$.\n\nLet's analyze the operation. We want to cover all 'B's with intervals of length $k$. Each interval costs 1 operation. We want to minimize the number of intervals.\nThis is equivalent to finding a minimum set of intervals of length $k$ such that every 'B' is covered by at least one interval.\nWait, is it exactly that? The operation turns cells to white. If we cover a 'B', it becomes 'W'. If we cover an already 'W' cell, it stays 'W'. So yes, covering all 'B's is sufficient. Overlapping intervals are allowed but might be redundant. We want to minimize the count.\n\nThis is a classic greedy interval covering problem.\nWe scan from left to right. When we encounter a 'B' that hasn't been covered yet, we must cover it. To maximize the benefit of this operation, we should place the interval of length $k$ starting at this 'B' (or as far right as possible while still covering this 'B').\nActually, if we have a 'B' at index $i$ (0-indexed) that is not yet covered, the optimal move is to apply the 